WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.047 --> 00:00:16.265
It was neither the season nor the hour when the park had visitors, and it is likely that the young lady, who was seated on one of the benches at the side of the walk, had merely obeyed a sudden impulse to sit for a while and enjoy the prospect of coming spring.

2
00:00:16.266 --> 00:00:19.290
She rested there, pensive and still.

3
00:00:19.291 --> 00:00:32.248
A certain melancholy in her expression must have been of recent birth, for it had not yet altered the fine and youthful lines of her cheek, nor tamed the mischievous though resolute curve of her lips.

4
00:00:32.249 --> 00:00:37.554
A tall young man came striding through the park along the path near which she sat.

5
00:00:37.555 --> 00:00:41.249
Behind him tagged along a boy carrying a suitcase.

6
00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:47.058
At the sight of the young lady, the man’s face changed to red and back to pale again.

7
00:00:47.059 --> 00:00:52.507
He watched her face as he drew nearer, with hope and anxiety mingled on his own.

8
00:00:52.508 --> 00:01:00.802
He passed within a few yards of her, but he saw no evidence that she was aware of his presence or existence.

9
00:01:00.803 --> 00:01:07.396
Some fifty yards further on, he suddenly stopped and sat on a bench at one side.

10
00:01:07.397 --> 00:01:12.616
The boy dropped the suitcase and stared at him with wondering, shrewd eyes.

11
00:01:12.617 --> 00:01:16.414
The young man took out his handkerchief and wiped his brow.

12
00:01:16.415 --> 00:01:21.975
It was a good handkerchief, a good brow, and the young man was good to look at.

13
00:01:21.976 --> 00:01:27.640
He said to the boy: "I want you to take a message to that young lady on that bench.

14
00:01:27.641 --> 00:01:35.815
Tell her I am on my way to the station, to leave for San Francisco, where I shall join that Alaska moose-hunting expedition.

15
00:01:35.816 --> 00:01:46.572
Tell her that since she has commanded me neither to speak nor to write to her, I take this means of making one last appeal to her sense of justice, for the sake of what has been.

16
00:01:46.573 --> 00:01:59.046
Tell her that to condemn and discard one who has not deserved such treatment, without giving him her reasons or a chance to explain, is contrary to her nature as I believe it to be.

17
00:01:59.047 --> 00:02:08.250
Tell her that I have, to a certain degree, disobeyed her orders, in the hope that she may yet be willing to see justice done.

18
00:02:08.251 --> 00:02:10.733
Go, and tell her that."

19
00:02:10.734 --> 00:02:14.485
The young man dropped a half-dollar into the boy’s hand.

20
00:02:14.486 --> 00:02:21.975
The boy looked at him for a moment with bright eyes out of a dirty, intelligent face, and then set off at a run.

21
00:02:21.976 --> 00:02:26.790
He approached the lady on the bench a little doubtfully, but unembarrassed.

22
00:02:26.791 --> 00:02:31.525
He touched the brim of the old plaid cap perched on the back of his head.

23
00:02:31.526 --> 00:02:36.141
The lady looked at him coolly, without prejudice or favor.

24
00:02:36.142 --> 00:02:42.233
"Lady," he said, "that guy on the other bench sent me to give you a song and dance.

25
00:02:42.234 --> 00:02:48.582
If you don’t know him, and he’s bothering you, just say the word, and I’ll have a cop over here in three minutes.

26
00:02:48.583 --> 00:02:53.746
If you do know him, and he’s telling the truth, I’ll tell you what he wanted me to tell you."

27
00:02:53.747 --> 00:02:56.452
The young lady showed a faint interest.

28
00:02:56.453 --> 00:03:04.928
"A song and dance!" she said, in a sweet voice that seemed to clothe her words in a barely noticeable layer of irony.

29
00:03:04.929 --> 00:03:09.234
"A new idea—like a singing telegram, I suppose.

30
00:03:09.235 --> 00:03:16.777
I . . . used to know the gentleman who sent you, so I think it will hardly be necessary to call the police.

31
00:03:16.778 --> 00:03:21.333
You may execute your song and dance, but do not sing too loudly.

32
00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:26.920
It is a little early yet for open-air performances, and we might attract attention."

33
00:03:26.921 --> 00:03:32.749
"Aw," said the boy, with a shrug down the length of him, "you know what I mean, lady.

34
00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:37.342
It’s not a real song and dance, it’s just an expression.

35
00:03:37.343 --> 00:03:43.236
He told me to tell you he’s got all his stuff packed into that suitcase to leave for San Francisco.

36
00:03:43.237 --> 00:03:46.593
Then, he’s going to shoot some snowbirds in the Klondike.

37
00:03:46.594 --> 00:03:54.459
He says you told him not to send any more notes or come to see you in your garden, and he’s using me to set the record straight.

38
00:03:54.460 --> 00:04:02.559
He says you judged him, and never even gave him a chance to change your mind. He says you left him, and never said why."

39
00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:07.318
The slightly awakened interest in the young lady’s eyes did not lessen.

40
00:04:07.319 --> 00:04:18.495
Perhaps it was caused by either the originality or the audacity of the snowbird hunter, who was bypassing her commands against the ordinary modes of communication in this way.

41
00:04:18.496 --> 00:04:25.463
She fixed her eye on a cheerless statue standing in the disheveled park, and spoke into the transmitter:

42
00:04:25.464 --> 00:04:34.240
"Tell the gentleman that I need not repeat to him a description of my ideals. He knows what they have been and what they still are.

43
00:04:34.241 --> 00:04:40.798
So far as they relate to this case, absolute loyalty and truth are the ones paramount.

44
00:04:40.799 --> 00:04:49.043
Tell him that I have studied my own heart as well as one can, and I know its weaknesses as well as I do its needs.

45
00:04:49.044 --> 00:04:52.921
That is why I decline to hear his pleas, whatever they may be.

46
00:04:52.922 --> 00:04:59.682
I did not condemn him because of a rumor or doubtful evidence, and that is why I made no charge.

47
00:04:59.683 --> 00:05:06.268
But, since he persists in hearing what he already well knows, you may convey the matter.

48
00:05:06.269 --> 00:05:12.281
"Tell him that I entered the greenhouse that evening from the rear, to cut a rose for my mother.

49
00:05:12.282 --> 00:05:17.368
Tell him I saw him and Miss Ashburton beneath the pink oleander.

50
00:05:17.369 --> 00:05:24.598
The scene was pretty, but the pose and juxtaposition were too eloquent and evident to require explanation.

51
00:05:24.599 --> 00:05:30.614
I left the greenhouse, and at the same time, the rose and my illusions of ideal love.

52
00:05:30.615 --> 00:05:34.037
You may carry that song and dance to your boss."

53
00:05:34.038 --> 00:05:40.153
"I'm not clear on one word, lady. Jux—jux—explain that, will you?"

54
00:05:40.154 --> 00:05:49.956
"Juxtaposition—or you may call it proximity—or, if you like, being rather too near for one maintaining the position of an ideal."

55
00:05:49.957 --> 00:05:54.939
The gravel spun from beneath the boy’s feet. He stood by the other bench.

56
00:05:54.940 --> 00:06:02.517
The man’s eyes interrogated him hungrily. The boy’s were shining with the impersonal eagerness of the translator.

57
00:06:02.518 --> 00:06:13.145
"The lady says that she knows that girls are often easy to fool when a fellow comes along telling them fairy tales and trying to make up, and that’s why she won’t listen to any nonsense.

58
00:06:13.146 --> 00:06:17.661
She says she caught you in the act, hugging some girl in the hothouse.

59
00:06:17.662 --> 00:06:22.901
She stopped by to get some flowers and you were squeezing some other girl really tight.

60
00:06:22.902 --> 00:06:29.258
She says it looked cute all right, but it made her sick. She says you’d better get going to catch your train."

61
00:06:29.259 --> 00:06:34.875
The young man gave a low whistle and his eyes flashed with a sudden understanding.

62
00:06:34.876 --> 00:06:40.050
His hand flew to the inside pocket of his coat, and drew out a handful of letters.

63
00:06:40.051 --> 00:06:46.800
Selecting one, he handed it to the boy, following it with a silver dollar from his vest pocket.

64
00:06:46.801 --> 00:06:54.324
"Give that letter to the lady," he said, "and ask her to read it. Tell her that it should explain the situation.

65
00:06:54.325 --> 00:07:01.422
Tell her that if she had mingled a little trust with her conception of the ideal, much heartache might have been avoided.

66
00:07:01.423 --> 00:07:08.150
Tell her that the loyalty she prizes so much has never wavered. Tell her I am waiting for an answer."

67
00:07:08.151 --> 00:07:10.429
The messenger stood before the lady.

68
00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:13.342
"The guy says he’s been dumped for no good reason.

69
00:07:13.343 --> 00:07:19.896
He says he’s not a bad guy, and lady, you should read that letter, and I’ll bet you he’s telling the truth, all right."

70
00:07:19.897 --> 00:07:24.753
The young lady unfolded the letter, somewhat doubtfully, and read it.

71
00:07:24.754 --> 00:07:26.512
Dear Dr. Arnold:

72
00:07:26.513 --> 00:07:39.090
I want to thank you for your most kind and opportune aid to my daughter last Friday evening, when she was overcome by an attack of her old heart trouble in the greenhouse at Mrs. Waldron’s reception.

73
00:07:39.091 --> 00:07:45.405
Had you not been near to catch her as she fell and to render proper attention, we might have lost her.

74
00:07:45.406 --> 00:07:49.267
I would be glad if you would call and check on the treatment of her case.

75
00:07:49.268 --> 00:07:50.690
Gratefully yours,

76
00:07:50.691 --> 00:07:52.656
Robert Ashburton

77
00:07:52.657 --> 00:07:56.667
The young lady refolded the letter, and handed it to the boy.

78
00:07:56.668 --> 00:08:01.310
"The fellow wants an answer," said the messenger. "What’s your reply?"

79
00:08:01.311 --> 00:08:06.989
The lady’s eyes suddenly flashed on him, bright, smiling, and wet.

80
00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:14.695
"Tell that guy on the other bench," she said, with a happy, trembling laugh, "that his girl wants him."

